// GitHub issue #479: "no other core is inside this region", as a value.
//
// WHAT THIS IS FOR
//
// The one defect this kernel's multicore work produced that the type
// system did not catch was this shape: core 0 destroyed a resource while
// core 1 might still hold a reference to it. Concretely, a probe cleared
// an `armed` flag and immediately reaped the process it had been racing
// for -- but clearing a flag stops the other core at its next CONDITION
// CHECK, so a core already inside the loop finishes one more iteration
// against a record being torn down. It appeared as one boot in eight
// reporting `records MISSING`, was silent in the other seven, and took a
// rate measurement on both sides of the change to attribute at all.
//
// The fix was two atomic booleans and a comment. This is that protocol
// with a type, so the next one is a missing argument instead of a flake.
//
// HOW IT READS
//
//     let visit = occupancy_enter(&region);        // on the other core
//     ... work ...
//     occupancy_leave(visit, &region);
//
//     match occupancy_drain(&region, LIMIT) {      // on this core
//         OccupancyDrainResult::Busy => { ... give up, say so ... }
//         OccupancyDrainResult::Empty(proof) => {
//             teardown(proof);                     // needs the proof
//             quiesced_release(proof);
//         }
//     }
//
// `Quiesced[region]` is linear and is the only way to call a teardown that
// declares `borrow Quiesced[region]`. So "reap before the other core has
// left" stops being a thing a reviewer has to notice.
//
// NOT A LOCK, AND NOT A COUNTER
//
// Each core writes ONLY its own word, so there is no read-modify-write
// here and nothing to exclude. That is deliberate twice over. A counter
// would need an atomic fetch-subtract, which this language does not have.
// And the paths this exists for are the paths where a lock is the
// WRONG answer -- the same reason kernel/lib/diagnostic_ring.tkb is
// per-CPU and Linux's printk ringbuffer is lock-free: a mechanism that
// runs while something is being torn down cannot block on the thing
// tearing it down.
//
// It is NetBSD's `CPUF_PAUSE` in a type. `sys/arch/amd64/amd64/
// db_interface.c` sets that flag per cpu_info and spins on it, and
// `db_mach_cpu` REFUSES to inspect a CPU that does not have it -- the same
// "you may only touch what you have stopped" rule, checked at runtime
// there and at compile time here.
//
// Current limitations (2026-08-30). Keep this list current as call sites
// change; a limitation that goes stale here is worse than none, since it
// invites relying on a guarantee that quietly stopped holding.
// - IT PROVES ABSENCE, NOT EXCLUSION. `Quiesced` says every core had left
//   at the moment of the drain. Nothing stops one entering again
//   afterwards -- the caller has to be the reason that cannot happen. In
//   the scheduler probe, clearing `armed` alone was NOT enough: another
//   core could have observed its old value before the clear and enter late.
//   Its locked path therefore rechecks the gate while holding the same lock
//   as teardown. A region without an equivalently ordered closed gate needs
//   a lock, not this.
// - THE DRAIN IS A BOUNDED SPIN, in iterations rather than in time,
//   because this file has no timer -- kernel/lib/ is linked by hosted
//   builds that have no CNTPCT. A caller that wants a deadline converts
//   one. `Busy` on expiry is a report, never a fail-stop: a core that
//   never leaves is exactly the situation where blocking forever is the
//   worst available answer.
// - ONE WORD PER CORE, sized by KERNEL_MAX_CORES. A core outside that
//   range cannot record its presence, which would make a drain lie. The
//   bound is checked, and out-of-range folds to core 0 -- wrong but
//   bounded, the same trade kernel/kernel/process.tkb's execution_here()
//   makes.
// - NO NESTING. Entering twice on one core writes the same word twice and
//   leaving once clears it, so a drain could succeed with that core still
//   inside. The linear `Occupying` makes the SECOND enter visible (two
//   live tokens), but nothing rejects it. Every caller today enters once.
// - WHOLE-MACHINE MEMBERSHIP (2026-10-07). CPU_ON reservations join the
//   possible-participant mask before firmware may expose the CPU. Start and
//   stop share the claim gate. Only definitive absence removes a new member;
//   firmware status truthfulness, holding-pen entry, atomic ordering and raw
//   mints remain trusted. WorldStopped is only a caller-selected subset;
//   MachineStopped retains the captured participant authority for full reads.
// - WORLD STOP IS ONE-DEEP. A nonblocking test-and-set gate refuses competing
//   initiators with Busy; it never waits for a CPU it may need to stop.
//   Request generations are never reused, including on integer exhaustion:
//   exhaustion refuses further requests rather than accepting an old ack.
//   These guarantees were revised on 2026-09-08 for repeated interval stops.
use "kernel/lib/atomic_word.tkb";
use "kernel/lib/execution_model.tkb";
use "kernel/lib/intrusive_pool.tkb";

// Shared bounded wait for diagnostics and their held-pool regression.
const DDB_WORLD_STOP_SPINS: usize = 10000000;

// Presence, per core. Private, so no other file can read or write it
// except through the four entry points below -- which is what makes the
// protocol the type rather than the convention.
struct Occupancy {
    private inside: [AtomicWord; KERNEL_MAX_CORES];
}

// Held by a core while it is inside the region. Linear, so leaving is not
// optional and is checked on every path including early returns -- which
// is the half of this protocol a hand-written flag is worst at.
linear struct Occupying[region: addr] {
    private cpu: usize;
}

// Proof that no core was inside the region. Linear so it cannot be
// duplicated or conjured; the only source is a drain that observed every
// core absent.
linear struct Quiesced[region: addr] {
    private cores: usize;
}

// Indexed by the region, for the reason IntrusivePoolInsertResult is
// indexed by its pool (GitHub issue #345): without it the identity is lost
// at exactly the boundary that matters, since a drain that can fail must
// return a variant.
must_use variant OccupancyDrainResult[region: addr] {
    Busy;
    Empty(Quiesced[region]);
}

// A complete stop is the only proof accepted by readers which replace a lock
// with machine quiescence. A partial stop carries only the obligation to
// resume cores that did acknowledge; its different type prevents it from
// being passed to such a reader.
struct WorldStop {
    private claimed: AtomicWord;
    private owner: AtomicWord;
    private generation: usize;
    private requested: AtomicWord;
    private ack: [AtomicWord; KERNEL_MAX_CORES];
    private participants: AtomicWord;
}

linear struct WorldStopped[stop: addr] {
    private mask: usize;
    private owner: usize;
}

linear struct WorldStopPartial[stop: addr] {
    private mask: usize;
    private owner: usize;
    private stopped_mask: usize;
}

must_use variant WorldStopResult[stop: addr] {
    Busy;
    Partial(WorldStopPartial[stop]);
    Complete(WorldStopped[stop]);
}

// The current possible-participant set is captured only while the controller
// gate is held. Pending and uncertain CPU_ON targets remain in this mask.
private linear struct CpuParticipants[stop: addr] {
    private mask: usize;
}

// Whole-machine inspection cannot accept a caller-selected subset stop.
linear struct MachineStopped[stop: addr] {
    private participants: CpuParticipants[stop];
    private owner: usize;
    private irq_flags: usize;
}

// A pool view stamped with the stop context. Its own lifetime must end
// before resume or destruction, even if no payload pointer was yet derived.
private linear struct MachineSlotView[stop: addr, pool: addr] {
    private live: IntrusiveSlotView[pool];
}

must_use variant MachineSlotProbe[stop: addr, pool: addr] {
    Missing;
    Live(MachineSlotView[stop, pool]);
}

linear struct MachineStopPartial[stop: addr] {
    private partial: WorldStopPartial[stop];
    private irq_flags: usize;
}

must_use variant MachineStopResult[stop: addr] {
    Busy;
    Partial(MachineStopPartial[stop]);
    Complete(MachineStopped[stop]);
}

// Firmware CPU_ON can be issued only while this reservation holds the same
// gate as a machine stop, after its target has joined the participant mask.
linear struct CpuStart[stop: addr] {
    private core: {0..<KERNEL_MAX_CORES as usize};
    private was_present: bool;
    private irq_flags: usize;
}

must_use variant CpuStartResult[stop: addr] {
    Busy;
    Reserved(CpuStart[stop]);
}

// One machine-wide controller. Its address is the type index carried by every
// token, so a proof cannot be spent against another stop domain. Production
// KERNEL_ACTIVE_CORES is a storage/scheduler bound, not runtime membership.
// Whole-machine callers use world_stop_machine_begin; subset probes may
// include an offline slot to exercise the partial result.
let mut kernel_world_stop: WorldStop;

// Called after the platform interrupt dispatcher has acknowledged the SGI
// but before it touches ordinary kernel state. The interrupt remains active
// until release. The holding pen spins rather than using interrupt_wait:
// interrupt handlers cannot carry may_block, and this is a bounded diagnostic
// or rollover interval rather than an idle state.
fn world_stop_interrupt_hold() !{interrupt, unsafe} {
    world_stop_hold(&kernel_world_stop);
}

private fn world_stop_hold(stop: *WorldStop @ id) !{interrupt, unsafe} {
    let generation: usize =
        atomic_word_load(&(*stop).requested);
    if (generation == 0) { return; }
    let raw_cpu: usize = cpu_id();
    if (raw_cpu >= KERNEL_MAX_CORES) { return; }
    let cpu: {0..<KERNEL_MAX_CORES as usize} = raw_cpu;
    let owner: usize = atomic_word_load(&(*stop).owner);
    if (owner == raw_cpu + 1) { return; }
    atomic_word_store(&(*stop).ack[cpu], generation);
    while (atomic_word_load(&(*stop).requested) ==
               generation) {
    }
    atomic_word_store(&(*stop).ack[cpu], 0);
}

// Stop every other core in [0, cores). The platform sends one SGI to the
// computed target set. A bounded wait returns Partial rather than claiming a
// proof when any target did not acknowledge.
private fn world_stop_claim(stop: *WorldStop @ id) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    return atomic_word_swap(&(*stop).claimed, 1);
}

private fn world_stop_begin_config(stop: *WorldStop @ id, cores: usize,
                                   spins: usize, notify: bool,
                                   stale_ack_probe: bool)
        -> WorldStopResult[id] !{unsafe} {
    let raw_owner: usize = cpu_id();
    if (cores == 0 || cores > KERNEL_MAX_CORES || raw_owner >= cores ||
            raw_owner >= KERNEL_MAX_CORES) { return WorldStopResult::Busy; }
    if (world_stop_claim(stop) != 0) { return WorldStopResult::Busy; }
    let mask: usize = (1 << cores) - 1;
    return world_stop_begin_claimed(stop, mask, spins, notify, stale_ack_probe);
}

// The caller owns the claim gate, including on every refusal return.
private fn world_stop_begin_claimed(stop: *WorldStop @ id, mask: usize,
                                    spins: usize, notify: bool,
                                    stale_ack_probe: bool)
        -> WorldStopResult[id] !{unsafe} {
    let raw_owner: usize = cpu_id();
    let previous: usize = (*stop).generation;
    if (previous == ~(0 as usize)) {
        atomic_word_store(&(*stop).claimed, 0);
        return WorldStopResult::Busy;
    }
    let generation: usize = previous + 1;
    (*stop).generation = generation;
    // Do not clear peer-owned ack words here. An old handler may still be
    // leaving; only an acknowledgement of THIS generation proves it stopped
    // for this request. Its old holding loop also exits if a newer request
    // overtakes the zero written by release.
    atomic_word_store(&(*stop).owner, raw_owner + 1);
    atomic_word_store(&(*stop).requested, generation);
    // Negative control: model a delayed acknowledgement from the previous
    // request AFTER this request was published. Never used with notification.
    if (stale_ack_probe) {
        for cpu: usize in 0..<KERNEL_MAX_CORES {
            if ((mask & (1 << cpu)) != 0 && cpu != raw_owner) {
                atomic_word_store(&(*stop).ack[cpu], previous);
            }
        }
    }
    if (notify) { platform_world_stop_notify(mask, raw_owner); }

    let mut remaining: usize = spins;
    let mut stopped_mask: usize = 0;
    let mut complete: bool = false;
    while (complete == false && remaining != 0) {
        remaining = remaining - 1;
        complete = true;
        stopped_mask = 0;
        for cpu: usize in 0..<KERNEL_MAX_CORES {
            if ((mask & (1 << cpu)) != 0 && cpu != raw_owner) {
                if (atomic_word_load(&(*stop).ack[cpu]) != generation) {
                    complete = false;
                } else {
                    stopped_mask = stopped_mask | (1 << cpu);
                }
            }
        }
    }
    if (complete) {
        let mut proof: WorldStopped[id] = { mask, raw_owner };
        return WorldStopResult::Complete(proof);
    }
    let mut partial: WorldStopPartial[id] = {
        mask, raw_owner, stopped_mask
    };
    return WorldStopResult::Partial(partial);
}

fn world_stop_begin(stop: *WorldStop @ id, cores: usize, spins: usize)
        -> WorldStopResult[id] !{unsafe} {
    return world_stop_begin_config(stop, cores, spins, true, false);
}

// Capture under the claim, never from an unlocked online-count read. The
// bootstrap CPU is present even before the first secondary reservation.
fn world_stop_machine_begin(spins: usize)
        -> MachineStopResult[&kernel_world_stop]
        !{unsafe, irq_masking_guard, lock_guard_40_world_stop} {
    let irq_flags: usize = mutex_irq_save();
    if (world_stop_claim(&kernel_world_stop) != 0) {
        mutex_irq_restore(irq_flags);
        return MachineStopResult::Busy;
    }
    let mask: usize = atomic_word_load(&kernel_world_stop.participants) | 1;
    let owner: usize = cpu_id();
    if (owner >= KERNEL_MAX_CORES || (mask & (1 << owner)) == 0) {
        atomic_word_store(&kernel_world_stop.claimed, 0);
        mutex_irq_restore(irq_flags);
        return MachineStopResult::Busy;
    }
    let mut participants: CpuParticipants[&kernel_world_stop] = { mask };
    match world_stop_begin_claimed(&kernel_world_stop, mask, spins, true, false) {
        WorldStopResult::Busy => {
            cpu_participants_end(participants);
            mutex_irq_restore(irq_flags);
            return MachineStopResult::Busy;
        }
        WorldStopResult::Partial(partial) => {
            cpu_participants_end(participants);
            let mut pending: MachineStopPartial[&kernel_world_stop] = { partial, irq_flags };
            return MachineStopResult::Partial(pending);
        }
        WorldStopResult::Complete(stopped) => {
            let owner: usize = stopped.owner;
            world_stopped_end(stopped);
            let mut machine: MachineStopped[&kernel_world_stop] = { participants, owner, irq_flags };
            return MachineStopResult::Complete(machine);
        }
    }
}

private inline fn cpu_participants_end(participants: sink CpuParticipants[id]) {}

fn machine_stop_release(machine: sink MachineStopped[id], stop: *WorldStop @ id)
        !{unsafe} {
    let irq_flags: usize = machine.irq_flags;
    cpu_participants_end(machine.participants);
    world_stop_resume(stop);
    mutex_irq_restore(irq_flags);
}

fn machine_stop_partial_release(pending: sink MachineStopPartial[id], stop: *WorldStop @ id)
        !{unsafe} {
    let irq_flags: usize = pending.irq_flags;
    world_stop_partial_release(pending.partial, stop);
    mutex_irq_restore(irq_flags);
}

fn machine_stop_partial_mask(pending: borrow MachineStopPartial[id]) -> usize {
    return world_stop_partial_mask(pending.partial);
}

// Terminal shutdown has no subsequent reader and never resumes the peers.
fn machine_stop_keep_forever(machine: sink MachineStopped[id]) {
    cpu_participants_end(machine.participants);
}

fn machine_stopped_mask(machine: borrow MachineStopped[id]) -> usize {
    let mut mask: usize = 0;
    for cpu: usize in 0..<KERNEL_MAX_CORES {
        if ((machine.participants.mask & (1 << cpu)) != 0 && cpu != machine.owner) {
            mask = mask | (1 << cpu);
        }
    }
    return mask;
}

// The unlocked proof stays inside this boundary; callers receive a scope
// whose stop and pool identities the checker retains through every read.
fn machine_pool_probe(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                      stopped: borrow MachineStopped[stop], slot: usize)
        -> MachineSlotProbe[stop, pool_id] !{unsafe, irq_masking_guard} {
    match intrusive_pool_probe_slot_unproven(pool, slot) {
        IntrusiveSlotProbe::NoPayload => { return MachineSlotProbe::Missing; }
        IntrusiveSlotProbe::Live(live) => {
            let mut scoped: MachineSlotView[stop, pool_id] = { live };
            return MachineSlotProbe::Live(scoped);
        }
    }
}

fn machine_pool_payload(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                        scoped: borrow MachineSlotView[stop, pool_id])
        -> *T @ stop !{unsafe} {
    return intrusive_pool_payload_of(pool, scoped.live);
}

fn machine_pool_view_drop(source: sink MachineSlotView[stop, pool_id]) {
    intrusive_view_drop(source.live);
}

// Reserving a CPU changes the logical participant context. These annotations
// also reject starts in a caller retaining a machine authority or its loans.
fn cpu_start_reserve(core: {0..<KERNEL_MAX_CORES as usize})
        -> CpuStartResult[&kernel_world_stop]
        !{unsafe, irq_masking_guard, lock_guard_40_world_stop, changes_witness_CpuParticipants, changes_witness_MachineStopped,
          changes_witness_MachineSlotView,
          changes_witness_RegionInspection__AddressSpaceBacking,
          changes_witness_RegionInspection__ProcessFdContext,
          changes_witness_RegionInspection__FdBlock} {
    if (core == 0 || cpu_id() != 0) { return CpuStartResult::Busy; }
    let irq_flags: usize = mutex_irq_save();
    if (world_stop_claim(&kernel_world_stop) != 0) {
        mutex_irq_restore(irq_flags); return CpuStartResult::Busy;
    }
    let mask: usize = atomic_word_load(&kernel_world_stop.participants) | 1;
    let bit: usize = 1 << core;
    atomic_word_store(&kernel_world_stop.participants, mask | bit);
    let mut start: CpuStart[&kernel_world_stop] = { core, (mask & bit) != 0, irq_flags };
    return CpuStartResult::Reserved(start);
}

fn cpu_start_core(start: borrow CpuStart[id]) -> {0..<KERNEL_MAX_CORES as usize} {
    return start.core;
}

// Only PSCI INVALID_PARAMETERS establishes that this newly reserved target
// does not exist. Success, timeout, and every uncertain status keep it in the
// possible set, even before its Takibi handshake. Never remove an old member.
fn cpu_start_finish(start: sink CpuStart[&kernel_world_stop], status: usize)
        !{unsafe} {
    let core: usize = start.core;
    let was_present: bool = start.was_present;
    let irq_flags: usize = start.irq_flags;
    cpu_start_end(start);
    if (status == 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE && was_present == false) {
        let mask: usize = atomic_word_load(&kernel_world_stop.participants);
        atomic_word_store(&kernel_world_stop.participants, mask & ~(1 << core));
    }
    cpu_start_gate_release();
    mutex_irq_restore(irq_flags);
}

private fn cpu_start_gate_release()
        !{unsafe, changes_witness_CpuStart, changes_witness_CpuParticipants,
          changes_witness_MachineStopped, changes_witness_MachineSlotView,
          changes_witness_RegionInspection__AddressSpaceBacking,
          changes_witness_RegionInspection__ProcessFdContext,
          changes_witness_RegionInspection__FdBlock} {
    atomic_word_store(&kernel_world_stop.claimed, 0);
}

private inline fn cpu_start_end(start: sink CpuStart[id]) {}

must_use variant WorldStopClaimProbeResult {
    Busy;
    UnexpectedUnclaimed;
}

// Exercise the production claim gate while retaining a complete stop.
// Unlike another full begin, this cannot mint a second authority or publish
// a request that would revoke the borrowed stop. UnexpectedUnclaimed reports
// a broken claim invariant; it never authorizes another inspection.
fn world_stop_busy_claim_probe(stopped: borrow WorldStopped[id],
                               stop: *WorldStop @ id)
        -> WorldStopClaimProbeResult !{unsafe} {
    if (world_stop_claim(stop) != 0) {
        return WorldStopClaimProbeResult::Busy;
    }
    return WorldStopClaimProbeResult::UnexpectedUnclaimed;
}

// Test-only negative control: production callers cannot suppress the SGI.
// With a real peer online this deterministically yields Partial, proving that
// absence of an acknowledgement is not silently promoted to a full token.
fn world_stop_begin_nonack_probe(
        stop: *WorldStop @ id, cores: usize, spins: usize)
        -> WorldStopResult[id] !{unsafe} {
    return world_stop_begin_config(stop, cores, spins, false, false);
}

// A private controller with no live handler is required: this negative control
// deliberately impersonates a late peer write rather than sending an SGI.
fn world_stop_begin_stale_ack_probe(
        stop: *WorldStop @ id, cores: usize, spins: usize)
        -> WorldStopResult[id] !{unsafe} {
    return world_stop_begin_config(stop, cores, spins, false, true);
}

private fn world_stop_resume(stop: *WorldStop @ id)
        !{unsafe, changes_witness_WorldStopped, changes_witness_WorldStopPartial,
          changes_witness_MachineStopped, changes_witness_MachineStopPartial,
          changes_witness_CpuParticipants, changes_witness_CpuStart,
          changes_witness_MachineSlotView,
          changes_witness_RegionInspection__AddressSpaceBacking,
          changes_witness_RegionInspection__ProcessFdContext,
          changes_witness_RegionInspection__FdBlock} {
    atomic_word_store(&(*stop).requested, 0);
    // owner remains stable until the next successful claimant publishes it.
    // Releasing the gate last prevents a new publication being cleared here.
    atomic_word_store(&(*stop).claimed, 0);
}

// End the local authority before the physical resume boundary. These private
// consumers do not resume a CPU or permit an authority to escape its file.
private inline fn world_stopped_end(stopped: sink WorldStopped[id]) {}
private inline fn world_stop_partial_end(partial: sink WorldStopPartial[id]) {}

fn world_stop_release(stopped: sink WorldStopped[id],
                      stop: *WorldStop @ id) !{unsafe} {
    world_stopped_end(stopped);
    world_stop_resume(stop);
}

fn world_stop_partial_release(partial: sink WorldStopPartial[id],
                              stop: *WorldStop @ id) !{unsafe} {
    world_stop_partial_end(partial);
    world_stop_resume(stop);
}

// A fail-stop that stopped the world never resumes it (GitHub issue #619):
// the other cores stay in world_stop_interrupt_hold for as long as the
// machine is on. This spends the token without the release the ordinary
// callers owe, and is for that terminal path only.
fn world_stop_keep_forever(stopped: sink WorldStopped[id]) {}

// What DDB prints when it is refused a stop (GitHub issue #632): who holds
// it, which request is out, and what each core last acknowledged. Plain
// atomic loads, no token: a reporter takes nothing, and these words are only
// ever read and written atomically. The four are not one snapshot.
fn world_stop_reporter_word(which: usize) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    return world_stop_word_of(&kernel_world_stop, which);
}

private fn world_stop_word_of(stop: *WorldStop @ id, which: usize) -> usize
        !{unsafe} {
    if (which == 0) { return atomic_word_load(&(*stop).claimed); }
    if (which == 1) { return atomic_word_load(&(*stop).owner); }
    return atomic_word_load(&(*stop).requested);
}

fn world_stop_reporter_ack(cpu: {0..<KERNEL_MAX_CORES as usize}) -> usize
        !{unsafe} {
    return world_stop_ack_of(&kernel_world_stop, cpu);
}

private fn world_stop_ack_of(stop: *WorldStop @ id,
                             cpu: {0..<KERNEL_MAX_CORES as usize}) -> usize
        !{unsafe} {
    return atomic_word_load(&(*stop).ack[cpu]);
}

fn world_stop_partial_mask(partial: borrow WorldStopPartial[id]) -> usize {
    return partial.stopped_mask;
}

// The complete token does not need to store a second copy of its mask: every
// non-owner core in the requested mask acknowledged, exactly the set the SGI
// targeted and the set release resumes.
fn world_stopped_mask(stopped: borrow WorldStopped[id]) -> usize {
    let mut mask: usize = 0;
    for cpu: usize in 0..<KERNEL_MAX_CORES {
        if ((stopped.mask & (1 << cpu)) != 0 && cpu != stopped.owner) {
            mask = mask | (1 << cpu);
        }
    }
    return mask;
}

private fn occupancy_cpu() -> {0..<KERNEL_MAX_CORES as usize} {
    let raw: usize = cpu_id();
    if (raw >= KERNEL_MAX_CORES) { return 0; }
    return raw;
}

// Announce that this core is inside. Release ordering, so everything the
// caller did before is visible to a core that later observes the word --
// and, more to the point here, so a drain that sees 0 is seeing a core
// that has really finished.
fn occupancy_enter(region: *Occupancy @ id) -> Occupying[id] !{unsafe} {
    let cpu: {0..<KERNEL_MAX_CORES as usize} = occupancy_cpu();
    atomic_word_store(&(*region).inside[cpu], 1);
    let mut visit: Occupying[id] = { cpu };
    return visit;
}

fn occupancy_leave(visit: sink Occupying[id], region: *Occupancy @ id)
        !{unsafe} {
    let cpu: usize = visit.cpu;
    if (cpu < KERNEL_MAX_CORES) {
        let inside_cpu: {0..<KERNEL_MAX_CORES as usize} = cpu;
        atomic_word_store(&(*region).inside[inside_cpu], 0);
    }
}

// Wait, bounded, for every core to be outside.
//
// The bound is in iterations because this file has no clock. A caller with
// a deadline converts it; a caller without one picks a number and lives
// with `Busy`.
fn occupancy_drain(region: *Occupancy @ id, spins: usize)
        -> OccupancyDrainResult[id] !{unsafe} {
    let mut remaining: usize = spins;
    let mut empty: bool = false;
    while (empty == false && remaining != 0) {
        remaining = remaining - 1;
        let mut occupied: bool = false;
        for cpu: usize in 0..<KERNEL_MAX_CORES {
            if (atomic_word_load(&(*region).inside[cpu]) != 0) {
                occupied = true;
            }
        }
        if (occupied == false) { empty = true; }
    }
    if (empty == false) { return OccupancyDrainResult::Busy; }
    let mut proof: Quiesced[id] = { KERNEL_MAX_CORES };
    return OccupancyDrainResult::Empty(proof);
}

// Give the proof back. It carries no runtime resource -- what it carries
// is the obligation to have been obtained, and being linear is what makes
// that obligation real.
fn quiesced_release(proof: sink Quiesced[id]) {}
